1
00:00:14,050 --> 00:00:15,780
NBC Connecticut bought four

2
00:00:15,790 --> 00:00:17,482
American Silver Eagles online.

3
00:00:17,492 --> 00:00:20,540
A lab found all four were mostly copper.

4
00:00:36,070 --> 00:00:36,930
I'm Justin.

5
00:00:36,940 --> 00:00:37,861
I built EagleEye.

6
00:00:37,871 --> 00:00:39,432
Point your phone at a coin

7
00:00:39,442 --> 00:00:40,650
and it tells you what it is

8
00:00:40,660 --> 00:00:42,230
and whether it is genuine.

9
00:00:42,310 --> 00:00:43,820
Everything runs on the phone.

10
00:00:44,260 --> 00:00:45,867
Here we have three coins in a row

11
00:00:45,877 --> 00:00:47,420
to showcase our demonstration.

12
00:00:47,440 --> 00:00:50,180
The first one is a Silver Eagle.

13
00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:51,720
It isn't just Eagles, though.

14
00:00:51,730 --> 00:00:53,730
This one is a genuine Morgan coin

15
00:00:53,740 --> 00:00:55,500
and the last one is a fake.

16
00:00:55,510 --> 00:00:57,630
As you can see, our program is able

17
00:00:57,640 --> 00:00:59,270
to detect which one's which.

18
00:00:59,470 --> 00:01:00,800
How was it made, though?

19
00:01:00,910 --> 00:01:02,690
Let me guide you through the process.

20
00:01:03,640 --> 00:01:05,100
First of all, coins are filmed

21
00:01:05,110 --> 00:01:05,820
and then frames are

22
00:01:05,830 --> 00:01:06,870
extracted from the video

23
00:01:06,880 --> 00:01:08,100
giving us training data.

24
00:01:08,110 --> 00:01:11,730
This was around one hundred images per coin.

25
00:01:12,340 --> 00:01:13,890
Next, I trained a model to tell what

26
00:01:13,900 --> 00:01:16,070
genuine Silver Eagles, Morgans

27
00:01:16,120 --> 00:01:17,250
and Peace dollars look like

28
00:01:17,260 --> 00:01:19,460
using a Keras model and Teachable Machine.

29
00:01:21,430 --> 00:01:22,980
After that, I created the model

30
00:01:22,990 --> 00:01:24,590
to run easily on a phone

31
00:01:24,850 --> 00:01:25,710
and it's small and compact

32
00:01:25,720 --> 00:01:28,790
using only around 680 kilobytes of storage.

33
00:01:29,620 --> 00:01:31,290
Now we have a Swift camera app

34
00:01:31,300 --> 00:01:32,190
that finds the coin

35
00:01:32,200 --> 00:01:33,660
and crops it using OpenCV

36
00:01:33,670 --> 00:01:36,890
to a size of 224 by 224 pixels.

37
00:01:37,330 --> 00:01:38,430
Here's a full flowchart.

38
00:01:38,440 --> 00:01:41,300
It takes a live feed on the phone, finds it

39
00:01:41,860 --> 00:01:44,840
crops it, uses the model

40
00:01:44,890 --> 00:01:46,730
from before to score it

41
00:01:46,930 --> 00:01:49,200
and if the confidence is above 90%

42
00:01:49,210 --> 00:01:51,710
bingo, your coin is likely genuine.

43
00:01:53,050 --> 00:01:55,505
This is the whole flowchart

44
00:01:56,590 --> 00:01:58,910
and this was the whole process.

45
00:02:02,680 --> 00:02:04,380
Before landing on this pipeline

46
00:02:04,390 --> 00:02:06,320
I also tried a YOLO detector.

47
00:02:06,430 --> 00:02:08,340
I trained it on about 600 photos

48
00:02:08,350 --> 00:02:09,572
to tell real from fake.

49
00:02:09,582 --> 00:02:11,720
Its scores looked great

50
00:02:11,730 --> 00:02:14,722
but YOLO looks at the whole camera view

51
00:02:14,732 --> 00:02:15,970
background included.

52
00:02:15,980 --> 00:02:18,311
In my photos, every real coin was

53
00:02:18,321 --> 00:02:20,940
in a slab and every fake one was loose

54
00:02:20,950 --> 00:02:21,750
so it could learn the

55
00:02:21,760 --> 00:02:23,580
background instead of the coin.

56
00:02:23,590 --> 00:02:25,200
That's why the final app crops

57
00:02:25,210 --> 00:02:26,910
tightly to the coin first.

58
00:02:26,920 --> 00:02:29,520
Next, I'll retrain it on matched photos

59
00:02:29,530 --> 00:02:31,680
test it on coins it's never seen

60
00:02:31,690 --> 00:02:33,709
and bring it into the app.

61
00:02:34,030 --> 00:02:37,010
EagleEye is a screening tool, not a certificate.

62
00:02:37,060 --> 00:02:39,690
Right now it knows Silver Eagles, Morgans

63
00:02:39,700 --> 00:02:42,350
and Peace dollars, and more coins are next.

64
00:02:42,460 --> 00:02:44,840
Make sure to look twice before you pay once.

65
00:02:44,890 --> 00:02:45,800
Thank you.
